From a34a92f59ba6ca158db08b21ed13d7fb93a8999d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 22:13:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop ToNumeric copying a Number it was already handed, item
 3-1

The boxing-source census found ++ and -- to be 30.9% of everything the corpus
boxes -- 51.6% on NavierStokes, 80.4% on EarleyBoyer -- and exactly half of
that to be waste. ToNumeric coerces the operand of ++/-- and hands back the
coerced old value, and it minted unconditionally, so `n++` on a Number copied
the Number into a second, equal JSNumber.

Reusing it is sound because a JavaScript Number has no observable identity: it
compares by value, it cannot carry a property, and Object.is on two Numbers is
a value comparison. That is the argument the small-integer cache has rested on
since P2-2, where unrelated call sites are already handed the same instance.

The guard is primitive.IsNumber, not !primitive.IsBigInt. A String, a Boolean,
null and undefined all reach this line and all still have to be coerced, which
is the whole reason ToNumeric exists -- `"1"++` yields the Number 1, not the
String.

Measured on the corpus, one build, BROILER_JS_NUMERIC_UPDATE_REUSE the only
difference: 17 285 913 requests removed, 15.4%, against a census prediction of
17 281 232 -- the thing built is the thing measured to 0.03%. In allocations
it is 7 050 834, 9.4%, and the gap between the two is the small-integer cache.
Crypto removes 7.2 M requests and 5 117 boxes, because its updates are loop
counters inside [-128, 1024] where P2-2 was already answering them for free;
NavierStokes' indices run past that bound, so 71.4% of its removed requests
were real -- 6.76 M boxes, 23.0% of everything it allocates. EarleyBoyer
halves. Five coercions still mint on the reuse arm, which is the guard
discriminating rather than a leak: those operands are not Numbers.

NumericUpdateReuseTests: 9 fixtures, each on both settings of the switch, so
every one is a statement about JavaScript semantics rather than a description
of the fast path. Postfix and prefix results, the non-Number operands that
must still be coerced, NaN and the infinities, -0 asserted through both 1/x
and Object.is, a valueOf that runs exactly once, a getter read once with the
setter seeing the increment, BigInt, the Symbol TypeError, and an element
update. Plus the identity argument asserted rather than assumed, and a counter
invariant -- UnaryToNumeric + UnaryToNumericReused is equal on both arms and
only the split moves -- because "the box count went down" would otherwise be
consistent with the coercion having stopped happening.
